# ThreadSanitizer suppressions for Thunderbolt HT.
#
# AbpDeque's steal() and push() read/write buffer_[index] as plain (non-atomic)
# memory. Ordering comes entirely from the top_/bottom_ index protocol - the
# fences from Le, Pop, Cohen & Zappa Nardelli, "Correct and Efficient
# Work-Stealing for Weak Memory Models" (PPoPP 2013) - not from the element
# access itself. That element access is a data race by the strict letter of
# the C++ memory model, and TSan is right to see it that way: it tracks
# happens-before over addresses, and there is genuinely no synchronization
# edge on the buffer_[index] access in isolation.
#
# It is provably not a BUG, though. push()'s capacity guard
# (`b - t >= capacity_` => refuse) makes it algorithmically impossible for a
# thief's read index and the owner's write index to alias the same physical
# slot while `t < b` - which is the only condition under which a thief's
# speculative read in steal() executes at all. Aliasing would require the
# deque to hold at least `capacity_` live elements, which push() never
# permits. TSan cannot see this invariant; it only sees two plain accesses to
# the same shadow-memory address with no proven order between them.
#
# This is a suppression of one specific, reasoned-through pattern - not a
# license to add other plain shared-memory access to this file without the
# same analysis. See AbpDeque.hpp's own header comment and the steal()/push()
# bodies for the full argument.
race:thunderbolt::AbpDeque
